 Remote attach supports Linux and macOS hosts on x86_64 and aarch64. Herdr checks the remote platform, prefers a matching `herdr` already on the remote `PATH`, then checks common direct, Homebrew, mise, and Nix profile install paths. If no matching binary exists, interactive runs prompt to install one to `~/.local/bin/herdr`; non-interactive runs fail instead of modifying the host. If `~/.local/bin` is not on the remote `PATH`, Herdr warns after install.
 
-Native Windows `herdr --remote` is not part of the Windows beta. From Windows, SSH into the server and run `herdr` there.
+Native Windows clients can attach to those Linux and macOS hosts as well as Windows hosts. On a Windows host, the SSH user's `PATH` must resolve `herdr.exe`; Herdr starts that exact executable and never installs a remote binary automatically. Live handoff remains Unix-host-only.
 
-By default, `herdr --remote` runs remote setup and the bridge through a temporary SSH config that includes your SSH config first, then adds fallback keepalive settings and a private per-attach control socket for connection reuse. Existing user keepalive settings win. Set `[remote].manage_ssh_config = false` to use plain `ssh` without Herdr's generated config or control socket.
+On a remote attach from Windows, the default `ctrl+v` remote-image binding sends a registered PNG or DIB clipboard image through the existing client protocol, stages it on the remote host, and pastes the remote path. Dragging or pasting one absolute PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, or BMP path uses the same bounded transport. Text input falls through unchanged when the clipboard has no supported image. Change or disable the shortcut with `keys.remote_image_paste`.
+
+By default, `herdr --remote` runs remote setup and the bridge through a temporary SSH config that includes your SSH config first, then adds fallback keepalive settings. Existing user keepalive settings win. Unix clients also add a private per-attach control socket for connection reuse. Set `[remote].manage_ssh_config = false` to use plain `ssh` without Herdr's generated config or control socket.
+
+On Windows, the generated config includes `%USERPROFILE%\.ssh\config` and the system OpenSSH config when present. Herdr does not use SSH control-socket reuse on Windows, so password authentication can prompt for multiple setup commands. Use key authentication or load the key into Windows `ssh-agent` for a one-step attach.
 
 Remote attach uses your normal OpenSSH authentication. If the target uses a passphrase-protected key in a non-interactive shell, script, CI job, or mobile terminal that cannot show the passphrase prompt, load the key into ssh-agent first:
 
